00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:30.540
factor memory limits into their setup. Okay, let's pop the hood on the frozen tower approach to

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:37.120
multimodal embeddings. The real engineering win comes from keeping the original text towers frozen

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:44.820
and training only thin adapters on top. This preserves the exact text embedding space you already tuned

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:52.960
while adding new modalities at roughly 0.35 percent of total parameter cost. The adapters project

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:59.840
non-text features into the frozen language model input space. So inference latency grows only

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:06.520
by the cost of the new encoders plus a small projection step. Quality stays within a few points of

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:13.540
fully trained multimodal models on retrieval benchmarks because the heavy semantic work still happens in

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:20.520
the original text backbone. The main trade-off is that cross-modal alignment cannot exceed what the

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:28.400
frozen text space already encodes. If your downstream task needs tighter image text coupling, you

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:34.940
eventually pay for joint fine tuning. Reach for the frozen approach when you already have production

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:41.960
text embeddings and want to add images or audio without retraining or breaking existing indexes.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:48.800
